Certainly! Graphviz uses the DOT language, which is a plain text graph description language. Here's a simple example of a directed graph with three nodes and two edges:
```dot
digraph G {
    A -> B;
    B -> C;
}
```
In this example, `digraph G` declares a directed graph named "G". The nodes (A, B, and C) and edges (A -> B and B -> C) are specified within the curly braces `{}`.

You can customize the appearance of nodes and edges using various attributes. Here's an example with customized nodes and edges:
```dot
digraph G {
    node [shape=ellipse, color=blue, fontcolor=white];
    edge [color=red, fontsize=12];

    A [label="Node A"];
    B [label="Node B"];
    C [label="Node C"];

    A -> B [label="Edge A to B"];
    B -> C [label="Edge B to C"];
}
```
In this example, the `node` and `edge` lines define default attributes for all nodes and edges, such as shape, color, and font size. Individual nodes and edges can also have specific attributes, such as labels.

To render the DOT script as an image, you can use Graphviz command-line tools like `dot` or libraries for various programming languages. For example, you can save the DOT script in a file named "example.dot" and render it as a PNG image using the following command:
```bash
dot -Tpng example.dot -o example.png
```
This will generate a PNG image named "example.png" containing the visual representation of your graph.

