Yes, option 1 can be made more efficient by only adding the nudge value to the diagonal elements when negative eigenvalues are found. This way, you avoid altering the matrix unnecessarily. Here's an updated version of option 1:
```R
eigen_values <- eigen(K)$values

# Check if there are any negative eigenvalues
if (any(eigen_values < 0)) {
  nudge_value <- 1e-6
  K_nudged <- K + diag(nudge_value, nrow(K))
  result <- rmvnorm(n=1, mean=rep(0, nrow(x)), sigma=K_nudged, method="chol")
} else {
  result <- rmvnorm(n=1, mean=rep(0, nrow(x)), sigma=K, method="chol")
}
```
This way, the nudge value is only added to the diagonal elements when the matrix has negative eigenvalues. If the matrix is already positive semi-definite, the original matrix K will be used directly.

